E.append(cortex("cortex-supplementary-motor", "Supplementary motor cortex (juxtapositional lobule)", "frontal",
 "The supplementary motor area occupies the medial surface of the superior frontal gyrus in front of the paracentral lobule and above the cingulate sulcus, the medial part of Brodmann area 6. It prepares and initiates internally generated, sequenced and bimanual movements and speech, and it contains its own somatotopic map. Its unique clinical picture combines an acute contralateral akinesia with mutism after infarction, seizures that begin with a tonic posture of the arm and vocalization, and, when both sides are damaged, akinetic mutism; it is a territory of the anterior cerebral artery and a frequent site of parasagittal meningioma.",
 "Medial surface of the hemisphere above the cingulate sulcus and in front of the paracentral lobule, from roughly the level of the anterior commissure back to the precentral sulcus, extending a short way onto the superior convexity.",
 "The SMA proper plans movement sequences from memory and initiates them without an external cue, coordinates the two hands, and gates the start of speech; the pre-SMA in front of it selects between competing actions and inhibits unwanted ones. Both receive the ventrolateral thalamus, closing the motor loop of the basal ganglia, and project to the primary motor cortex, the striatum, the pontine nuclei and directly to the spinal cord. Its loss produces reduced spontaneous movement and speech that recover over weeks because the lateral premotor cortex takes over externally cued actions, whereas self-initiated actions remain slow.",
 ACA,
 [mview("sagittal", "cortex-supplementary-motor-l", "supplementary motor area on the medial superior frontal gyrus"), mview("coronal", "cortex-supplementary-motor-r", "medial frontal cortex above the cingulate sulcus")],
 "Medial frontal cortex above the cingulate sulcus in front of the paracentral lobule on sagittal images; the vertical line through the anterior commissure marks the boundary between pre-SMA and SMA proper.",
 [pathol("SMA syndrome after resection or infarct", "MRI", "Medial frontal lesion in front of the paracentral lobule; the patient has contralateral akinesia and mutism that resolve over weeks.", sequence="T1", timing="Deficit maximal in the first days, recovery over 2-6 weeks"),
  pathol("Supplementary motor seizures", "EEG", "Sudden asymmetric tonic posturing with the contralateral arm abducted and the head turned toward it, vocalization, short duration and clustering in sleep; interictal EEG often normal.", sequence="n/a"),
  pathol("Parasagittal meningioma", "MRI", "Dural mass along the superior sagittal sinus compressing the medial frontal cortex with focal motor seizures or paraparesis.", sequence="T1+Gd")],
 [("Contralateral akinesia and reduced spontaneous movement", "contralateral", "Loss of internally generated movement initiation from the SMA"), ("Mutism or reduced spontaneous speech", "n/a", "Loss of the SMA drive to speech initiation, especially with dominant-side lesions"), ("Alien hand and difficulty with bimanual tasks", "contralateral", "Loss of intermanual coordination and inhibition provided by the SMA and callosal fibers")],
 ["Compare self-initiated with cued movement: ask the patient to start an action versus imitate it", "Bimanual alternating tasks and the ability to start speaking spontaneously", "Seizure history: brief nocturnal tonic posturing with preserved awareness"],
 ["SMA syndrome is transient: warn the family before medial frontal surgery", "A fencing posture at seizure onset points to the supplementary motor area", "Akinetic mutism from bilateral medial frontal damage is not coma: the eyes follow"],
 [S(8, 284, 285), S(7, 258, 259), B(4, 33, 34), B(18, 168, 170)], synonyms=["SMA", "medial area 6"], territories=["territory-aca"], pathways=["pathway-lateral-corticospinal", "pathway-basal-ganglia-loops"], syndromes=["syn-aca-infarct"],
 afferents=[("Ventrolateral thalamus (basal ganglia output)", "superior thalamic radiation"), ("Prefrontal and cingulate cortex", "cingulum and short association fibers"), ("Contralateral SMA", "corpus callosum")],
 efferents=[("Primary motor cortex", "short association fibers"), ("Spinal cord and brainstem", "corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers"), ("Putamen", "corticostriatal fibers")],
 pitfalls=["SMA seizures are often labeled psychogenic because the EEG is normal and the movements are bizarre; video-EEG with an interhemispheric focus in mind is needed"], tags=["premotor", "SMA", "area 6"]))

E.append(cortex("gyrus-paracingulate", "Paracingulate gyrus", "frontal",
 "The paracingulate gyrus is a variable gyrus on the medial frontal surface between the cingulate sulcus and the paracingulate sulcus, present in most left hemispheres and fewer right ones, corresponding to the dorsal anterior cingulate and medial prefrontal cortex of Brodmann areas 32 and part of 24. It belongs to the cingulo-opercular control network that monitors performance, detects errors and conflict, and adjusts effort, and it is the medial frontal region most consistently activated by cognitive tasks. Its asymmetry is a normal variant, but reduced paracingulate folding has been linked to schizophrenia, and damage contributes to apathy and to impaired conflict resolution.",
 "Medial frontal surface above the anterior cingulate gyrus, between the cingulate sulcus below and the paracingulate sulcus above, running from the level of the genu back toward the supplementary motor area; when the paracingulate sulcus is absent the area is part of the superior frontal gyrus.",
 "Dorsal anterior cingulate and paracingulate cortex signals when an action conflicts with a goal or produces an error, recruits the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex to increase control, and weighs the effort and reward of a task; it drives the anterior insula in the salience network and contributes to the motivation to act and to the affective response to pain. Its projections to the striatum and the brainstem autonomic centers couple mental effort to heart rate and blood pressure. Bilateral damage with the neighboring anterior cingulate produces apathy and, when extensive, akinetic mutism.",
 ACA,
 [mview("sagittal", "gyrus-paracingulate-l", "paracingulate gyrus between the cingulate and paracingulate sulci"), mview("coronal", "gyrus-paracingulate-r", "medial frontal cortex above the anterior cingulate")],
 "On sagittal images a second sulcus parallel to and above the cingulate sulcus, the paracingulate sulcus, marks this gyrus; it is present on the left in about two-thirds of brains and is a normal asymmetry.",
 [pathol("Anterior cerebral artery infarct", "MRI", "Medial frontal restricted diffusion above the genu with apathy, reduced speech and contralateral leg weakness.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Cingulate glioma", "MRI", "FLAIR-bright infiltration of the medial frontal cortex crossing to the other side through the corpus callosum in a patient with apathy or seizures.", sequence="FLAIR"),
  pathol("Anterior cingulate seizures", "EEG", "Fearful screaming, complex hypermotor behavior and autonomic change from sleep with a poorly localized frontal EEG.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Apathy, reduced motivation and effort", "bilateral", "Loss of the effort-reward evaluation of the dorsal anterior cingulate"), ("Impaired error monitoring and conflict resolution", "bilateral", "Loss of the cingulate performance-monitoring signal to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex"), ("Blunted autonomic response to effort and pain", "bilateral", "Interruption of cingulate projections to the insula and brainstem autonomic centers")],
 ["Stroop and go/no-go tasks for conflict and inhibition", "Ask about initiative and interest; apathy scales help", "Look for accompanying leg weakness or grasp reflex indicating a wider medial frontal lesion"],
 ["A paracingulate sulcus on the left and not the right is a normal asymmetry, not atrophy", "The anterior cingulate is the cortex of effort: lesions make patients indifferent, not weak", "Hypermotor seizures from the anterior cingulate are dramatic and misdiagnosed as parasomnias"],
 [S(7, 259), S(9, 301, 306), B(7, 61, 62), B(18, 168, 170)], synonyms=["dorsal anterior cingulate cortex", "area 32"], territories=["territory-aca"], pathways=["pathway-papez-limbic"], syndromes=["syn-aca-infarct"],
 afferents=[("Anterior and mediodorsal thalamus", "anterior thalamic radiation"), ("Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex", "short association fibers"), ("Amygdala", "cingulum")],
 efferents=[("Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and SMA", "cingulum"), ("Anterior insula", "cingulo-opercular fibers"), ("Striatum and brainstem autonomic nuclei", "corticostriatal and descending fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Absence of the paracingulate sulcus on one side is common and must not be reported as a malformation"], tags=["anterior cingulate", "salience network", "area 32"]))

E.append(cortex("cortex-subcallosal", "Subcallosal cortex", "frontal",
 "The subcallosal cortex is the medial frontal cortex beneath the genu and rostrum of the corpus callosum, including the subgenual cingulate (Brodmann area 25), the paraterminal gyrus and the subcallosal area in front of the lamina terminalis. It is the part of the limbic cortex most closely tied to mood: it is overactive in depression, quiet after successful treatment and a target of deep brain stimulation for resistant depression. It connects the medial prefrontal cortex to the hypothalamus, amygdala and brainstem monoamine nuclei, lies against the anterior cerebral and anterior communicating arteries, and is bordered behind by the septal area and the basal forebrain, which is why anterior communicating aneurysm surgery can leave a patient amnestic and apathetic.",
 "Medial surface of the frontal lobe below the genu of the corpus callosum and in front of the lamina terminalis and the septal area, above the olfactory trigone and the anterior perforated substance, continuous with the gyrus rectus below.",
 "Subgenual cingulate cortex integrates the emotional value assigned by the orbitofrontal cortex with hypothalamic and visceral states and sets the tone of mood and the autonomic response to sadness; it inhibits the amygdala and modulates the raphe and locus coeruleus through descending fibers, and its metabolic activity tracks depressive symptoms. The paraterminal gyrus and subcallosal area belong to the septal region and take part in the reward and memory circuits through the fornix and the medial forebrain bundle. The region also processes olfactory and visceral information reaching it from the anterior perforated substance.",
 ACA + ["artery-acom"],
 [mview("sagittal", "cortex-subcallosal-l", "subcallosal cortex under the genu and rostrum"), mview("coronal", "cortex-subcallosal-r", "subgenual cingulate below the genu of the corpus callosum")],
 "Thin cortex under the genu on midline sagittal images, curving down in front of the lamina terminalis and the anterior commissure; the anterior cerebral arteries run immediately in front of it in the interhemispheric fissure.",
 [pathol("Anterior communicating artery aneurysm rupture", "CT", "Blood in the interhemispheric fissure with a hematoma of the subcallosal and gyrus rectus region; later basal forebrain infarcts with amnesia.", sequence="CT non-contrast"),
  pathol("Treatment-resistant depression (functional imaging)", "PET", "Increased metabolism in the subgenual cingulate that falls with effective treatment; used to plan deep brain stimulation.", sequence="n/a"),
  pathol("Low-grade glioma of the gyrus rectus and subcallosal area", "MRI", "Non-enhancing FLAIR-bright expansion below the genu with mood change and anosmia.", sequence="FLAIR")],
 [("Depressed mood, anhedonia and altered autonomic tone", "bilateral", "Dysfunction of the subgenual cingulate mood circuit and its hypothalamic and monoaminergic connections"), ("Apathy and amnesia after vascular injury", "bilateral", "Extension of anterior communicating artery infarction into the septal area and basal forebrain"), ("Anosmia", "ipsilateral", "Involvement of the adjacent olfactory trigone and tract")],
 ["Mood, anhedonia and sleep history in any patient with a medial frontal lesion", "Recent memory and confabulation after anterior communicating artery aneurysm repair", "Smell testing"],
 ["Area 25 is the hub of the depression circuit and a stimulation target; a lesion here can present as new depression", "The anterior communicating artery sits directly in front of the subcallosal cortex and septal area: rupture and clipping injure both", "Subcallosal and orbitofrontal lesions share the olfactory tract and cause anosmia"],
 [S(7, 259, 260), S(9, 301, 306), B(7, 61, 62), B(22, 236, 238)], synonyms=["subgenual cingulate", "area 25", "paraterminal gyrus"], territories=["territory-aca"], pathways=["pathway-papez-limbic", "pathway-olfactory"], syndromes=["syn-aca-infarct", "syn-wernicke-korsakoff"],
 afferents=[("Orbitofrontal and medial prefrontal cortex", "short association fibers"), ("Amygdala and hippocampus", "uncinate fasciculus and fornix"), ("Anterior perforated substance (olfactory)", "olfactory striae")],
 efferents=[("Hypothalamus", "medial forebrain bundle"), ("Raphe and locus coeruleus", "descending medial forebrain bundle fibers"), ("Ventral striatum and accumbens", "corticostriatal fibers")],
 pitfalls=["The subgenual region is small and easily overlooked on axial images; look at midline sagittal views when a mood or memory change follows an anterior circulation event"], tags=["subgenual cingulate", "mood", "area 25"]))

